  BSD-2-Clause into GPL-3.0-only, and MIT into GPL-3.0-only, are both ONE-WAY
  flows. None of these three files may be contributed back to its upstream
  under that upstream's licence without its author's agreement, and the same
  applies to anything derived from them here.

  EVERYTHING ELSE IN PETIR IS NOT DERIVED FROM EITHER CRATE. Outside those
  three files, no code here is copied from, translated from, or derived from
  `roots` or from `peroxide`; every other routine traces to the upstream named
  in its own file header -- GSL, ARM optimized-routines, OpenFOAM by way of
  outram-foam-basic-lib, or the GNU Octave control package by way of chem-eng
  -- and `tests/verbatim_provenance.rs` and the code-to-code reference sets
  under `reference-data/` are what hold that claim to account.

  NEITHER CRATE IS A DEPENDENCY. All three modules were ported, not linked, so
  `Cargo.toml` names neither crate; the obligations above are
  source-redistribution obligations, not dependency ones.

  WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY NOT PORTED, and why -- since both crates overlap PETIR
  far more widely than three files.

    The GSL-verified modules, from either crate. PETIR's maturity rests on
    bit-identity with GSL 2.8 compiled and executed; five of seven numerics
    surfaces agree with it exactly, every iterate. `roots`' Brent is not GSL's
    Brent, so swapping it in would break every one of those comparisons by
    construction and buy no capability. Every port above is ADDITIVE: each
    fills a gap GSL does not cover and replaces nothing that is verified.

    `roots`' find_roots_sturm, which is broken. Measured against upstream
    compiled and run (reference-data/roots/), it never returns more than three
    roots at any degree -- 3, 3, 3, 1, 3, 1, 1 for degrees 4 through 10 of
    prod(x-k) -- and reports no error when it drops the rest. A root finder
    that silently returns some of the roots is worse than none. A test pins
    that measurement so a future upstream fix gets noticed rather than the
    decision inherited.

    `peroxide` as a DEPENDENCY, which remains impossible: it pulls `blas`,
    `blas-src`, `lapack`, `lapack-src`, `netcdf`, `arrow`, `csv` and `json`
    across its feature set, and PETIR's crate rules forbid anything bringing
    system BLAS/LAPACK, a C or Fortran toolchain, `std`, threads, or an
    allocator beyond `alloc`. That has no bearing on PORTING from it: a
    pure-Rust routine copied across brings none of those with it, which is why
    dense.rs is here.

  It is recorded because credit for solving a problem first is not contingent
  on someone else having used your solution, and because a reader comparing
  PETIR against the Rust ecosystem should be pointed at the work that came
  before it rather than left to assume there was none.

  Both crates were EARLY to this in Rust -- doing the unglamorous work of
  getting real numerical methods into the language while the ecosystem was
  still thin, without which "just use the Rust crate" would not have been a
  sentence anyone could say. That is the part worth acknowledging, and it is
  not diminished by PETIR having different constraints.

  WHY PETIR EXISTS ALONGSIDE THEM, since that is the obvious next question:
  PETIR is `no_std` unconditionally and carries provenance to a specific
  upstream commit for every routine, because this workspace needs kernels that
  build for bare metal and wasm AND that a reviewer can diff line-for-line
  against a reference implementation with its own test suite. Those are
  constraints particular to this project, not deficiencies in either crate
  above.

